===========================================================================
 THE CULT  --  a hidden-role social-deduction game over UNIX stream sockets
 Author: Abhishek Singh
 Course: CSE/CEN Advanced Operating Systems, Arizona State University, Fall 2026
===========================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
------------
The Cult is a text-based, Werewolves-style game for a Linux machine. Each
player is a separate Linux user running a client process; a single server
process (the "Prophet") runs the game. Unlike Werewolves, nobody is ever
killed or removed -- a hidden Cult Leader CONVERTS players one at a time,
and converted players secretly join a growing "cult channel" while keeping
up appearances in public. The town tries to expose cultists by vote.

Because no player is ever eliminated, there is no "dead player still
connected" problem: a socket that stays open for the whole game is the
normal, expected case, which keeps the connection lifecycle simple.

All communication goes through a single-file communications library:

        cultnet.py        <-- THE COMMUNICATIONS LIBRARY (one file)

Nothing else in the project opens a socket or frames a message.


COMMUNICATIONS LIBRARY: cultnet.py
----------------------------------
Primitive: UNIX domain STREAM sockets (AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM), multiplexed
in one server process with a `selectors` (epoll) event loop. Messages are
length-prefixed JSON frames: a 4-byte big-endian length followed by that
many bytes of UTF-8 JSON. Peer identity is taken from the kernel via the
SO_PEERCRED socket option, so a client cannot forge who it is.

API -- SERVER SIDE (class CultServer):
    CultServer(sock_path)        Create, bind, and listen on a UNIX socket.
    .poll(timeout) -> [events]   Pump the event loop once. Returns a list of:
                                   ('connect',    cid, uid)
                                   ('message',    cid, obj)
                                   ('disconnect', cid)
    .send(cid, obj)              UNICAST one JSON object to one connection.
    .multicast(cids, obj)        MULTICAST the same object to a subset.
    .broadcast(obj)              BROADCAST the same object to everyone.
    .peers() -> [cid]            All currently connected connection ids.
    .uid_of(cid) -> uid          Kernel-verified Linux UID for a connection.
    .close()                     Shut everything down and remove the socket.

API -- CLIENT SIDE (class CultClient):
    CultClient(sock_path)        Connect to the server's UNIX socket.
    .send(obj)                   Send one JSON object (framed) to the server.
    .recv(timeout=None) -> obj   Receive one JSON object (None on close /
                                 timeout).
    .close()                     Disconnect.

"obj" is any JSON-serialisable dict. The library handles framing and
delivery only; the game layer decides what the keys mean.

Addressing vs. identity: each connection gets a unique integer id (cid)
used for delivery, and carries the kernel-verified UID (available via
uid_of). Keeping the two separate means several clients that share a UID
(e.g. a grader running five terminals as one user) are still distinct,
addressable players, while the game can still trust uid_of() for "who is
this really".


FILES
-----
  cultnet.py       THE communications library (single file). Both server
                   and client import it. Contains all socket/framing code.
  cultserver.py    The game server / moderator ("the Prophet"). Phases,
                   roles, conversions, votes, win conditions, logging.
  cultclient.py    The player client. Two threads: receive+print, and
                   read-input+send. Thin -- it knows no rules.
  cultbot.py       An automated bot player (optional). Connects exactly
                   like a human; useful for testing and for simulating a
                   full game for screenshots.
  cultconfig       Configuration (socket path, log dir, phase durations,
                   number of special roles, win fraction, etc.).
  roles.txt        Optional fixed role assignment (used when randomRoles=0
                   in cultconfig) for reproducible demos. Format: one
                   "name role" per line.
  makeplayers.py   Creates player0..playerN-1 Linux users (calls mkplayer.sh).
  mkplayer.sh      Creates one playerN user and installs the client files.
  install.sh       Full install: cultmaster user, player users, perms.
  uninstall.sh     Removes all the users this created.
  README           This file.


SETUP (on an Ubuntu Linux VM, with sudo)
-----------------------------------------
From inside the unpacked source directory:

    sudo ./install.sh 6          # create cultmaster + player0..player5

This creates:
  * a 'cultmaster' user owning the server, the socket, and the logs,
  * players player0 .. player5, each with cultclient.py and cultnet.py in
    their home directory,
  * /home/cultmaster/log, readable only by cultmaster (the MASTER log holds
    hidden roles and conversions; the PUBLIC log is the town transcript).

Edit /home/cultmaster/cultconfig if you want different timers or player
counts. minPlayers must be <= the number of players you created.


PLAYING
-------
1. Start the server as cultmaster (in one terminal):

       sudo -u cultmaster bash -c 'cd /home/cultmaster && python3 cultserver.py'

   It prints that it is listening and waits in the lobby for players.

2. For each player, open a terminal and become that user, then run the
   client (it finds the server at the socket path in cultconfig's default):

       sudo -u player0 python3 /home/player0/cultclient.py
       sudo -u player1 python3 /home/player1/cultclient.py
       ... and so on.

   (Give each client an explicit name if you like:
       python3 /home/player0/cultclient.py /home/cultmaster/cult.sock Alice )

3. Once minPlayers have joined (or joinTime expires), the game starts.

HOW TO PLAY (what to type in the client)
----------------------------------------
  * Just type text to CHAT. During the day it goes to everyone (broadcast);
    at night, if you are in the cult, it goes to the cult only (multicast).
  * If you are the Cult Leader, at night type:   pitch <name>
  * If you are an Investigator, at night type:   check <name>
  * During the day vote, type:                   vote <name>

The server tells you your role privately at the start and prompts you when
it is your turn to act.


QUICK LOCAL TEST (no user creation, single account)
---------------------------------------------------
You can exercise everything as one user with a local socket path. Make a
config pointing sockPath/logDir somewhere writable with short timers, start
the server, then start several clients (or bots) in other terminals against
that same socket path. Example with bots:

    python3 cultserver.py myconfig &
    python3 cultbot.py /tmp/cult.sock alice 1 &
    python3 cultbot.py /tmp/cult.sock bob   2 &
    ... (5+ bots) ...


UNINSTALL
---------
    sudo ./uninstall.sh 6


WHY THIS PRIMITIVE (vs. the named pipes in Werewolves)
------------------------------------------------------
  * One process selects over ALL clients at once, so the server never does
    a blocking read on one specific player's channel. There is no per-player
    "which pipe is the server waiting on" wait-state for an attacker to
    observe (the Werewolves pipe side channel).
  * SO_PEERCRED gives kernel-verified sender identity, so players cannot
    impersonate each other (the Werewolves imposter problem).
  * Messages are framed JSON written to the socket, never interpolated into
    a shell command, so the Werewolves command-injection class cannot occur.
